            "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. The M111.940 gives the late W124 E 200 a much more modern engine character than the older M102 units, because the 2.0-liter 16-valve four-cylinder feels smoother, cleaner, and more efficient without becoming overly complex by current standards. In the E 200, it delivers adequate rather than exciting performance, but it suits the car's durable executive-car character well. This is still a relatively simple naturally aspirated Mercedes petrol engine, yet age now matters as much as design quality. Oil leaks, vacuum integrity, ignition health, cooling-system condition, and wiring quality all have a major effect on ownership experience. A healthy M111.940 feels smooth, honest, and dependable. A neglected one feels flat, rough, and older than it should.",
            "injectionType": "PMS-controlled electronically managed multi-point petrol injection with integrated ignition control. The M111.940 relies on stable fueling, clean sensor signals, and good intake sealing to preserve its smooth and predictable response. Because it is a naturally aspirated 1990s Mercedes engine, it is less system-heavy than later models, but age-related vacuum leaks, weak ignition parts, and tired sensors can still noticeably affect drivability. When healthy, the engine feels linear and refined for its era. When fueling, air leaks, or ignition quality deteriorate, it quickly becomes uneven and less pleasant in everyday use.",
            "engineLifespan": "350,000 to 550,000 km. The M111.940 is capable of very strong long-term mileage when maintained correctly, because the basic engine design is robust, understressed, and well matched to the W124 platform. Regular oil changes, good coolant condition, ignition maintenance, timing-chain health, and attention to ageing rubber and wiring all influence ownership quality. Cars with documented servicing and sensible use often age extremely well. Poorly maintained examples usually become annoying rather than catastrophic at first, then gradually expensive as several small faults overlap.",
            "turboType": "Naturally aspirated inline-four petrol engine. The lack of turbocharging is one of the reasons the M111.940 can be such a sensible long-term engine, because it avoids many of the heat and boost-related failure points seen in later Mercedes drivetrains. Its character depends much more on clean fueling, healthy ignition, proper compression, and stable intake vacuum than on forced-induction hardware. This makes the engine simpler to own and easier to diagnose than newer turbocharged alternatives. It is not fast, but it is fundamentally honest and durable.",
