                "torque": "170 Nm",
                "fuelType": "Petrol",
                "cylinders": 4,
                "valves": 16,
                "compression": "9.6:1",
                "yearRange": "1993-1995",
                "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. This lower-output M111.940 profile represents a market-specific or fleet-oriented interpretation of the late W124 E 200 idea, where the modern 2.0-liter 16-valve engine is retained but the overall output is assumed to be softened for taxation, fleet, or export positioning. The result is a calmer and more deliberately understated version of the standard M111 E 200 character, with less urgency in the upper range and a greater emphasis on predictable daily usability than on outright performance. Even in this detuned form, the basic engine architecture remains much more modern and technically refined than the older M102 family, which means smoother running, cleaner combustion behavior, and better long-term drivability when the car is in good health. At the same time, age now matters just as much as engineering quality, because vacuum integrity, ignition quality, cooling condition, gasket health, and especially ageing engine-bay wiring can all shape how the car feels in the real world. A healthy example feels smooth, honest, and mechanically tidy. A neglected one can feel flat, inconsistent, and much older than the badge suggests.",
                "injectionType": "Electronically managed manifold petrol injection with PMS or export-market management logic, depending on the exact market calibration. In this lower-output profile, the engine still depends heavily on stable sensor signals, clean fueling behavior, intact intake sealing, and consistent ignition quality to preserve the refined character that distinguishes the M111 from earlier Mercedes four-cylinders. Because this is still a naturally aspirated 1990s Mercedes engine, it is easier to understand and usually easier to diagnose than later engines, but age-related vacuum leaks, poor replacement parts, weak coil or lead behavior, and tired sensors can still spoil drivability very quickly. When everything is healthy, the engine feels linear, civilized, and more modern than the older 8-valve units. When air, fuel, or ignition quality drifts, it becomes hesitant and uneven in a very un-premium way.",
                "engineLifespan": "330,000 to 520,000 km. The lower-output interpretation of the M111.940 should still be capable of excellent long-term mileage because the core engine design remains robust, understressed, and well suited to the relaxed character of a W124. Oil quality, timing-chain condition, coolant discipline, ignition maintenance, and age-related rubber and wiring condition matter more than the modest output figure itself. Cars with proper servicing and sensible use often age extremely well, especially if they avoid repeated overheating and cheap electrical repairs. Poorly maintained examples usually do not fail in one dramatic moment, but instead become progressively more annoying as several small faults overlap into one frustrating ownership experience. When looked after correctly, this is still the kind of engine that can deliver very high mileage with a fundamentally solid mechanical feel.",
                "turboType": "Naturally aspirated inline-four petrol engine. The absence of turbocharging is one of the biggest advantages of this profile because it keeps thermal stress, plumbing complexity, and long-term cost lower than on later forced-induction drivetrains. Its character depends much more on clean fueling, proper compression, healthy ignition parts, and stable intake vacuum than on boost hardware. That makes the engine simpler to own and easier to keep in good condition over the long term. It is not a fast configuration, but it is honest and mechanically transparent. In the right car, that simplicity is part of the appeal.",
                "ecuType": "Mercedes/PMS-style integrated petrol engine management controlling fueling, ignition timing, idle behavior, and key sensor strategies. The control system is far simpler than later Mercedes electronic packages, but it still depends on healthy wiring and accurate sensor input to deliver smooth drivability. Age-related wiring deterioration and vacuum issues can easily mimic bigger mechanical problems, which is why diagnosis still matters on these cars. In a sorted example, the engine feels smooth and tidy. In a tired one, it can feel strangely inconsistent, with symptoms that move around depending on temperature and load.",
                "euroStandard": "Euro 1 to early Euro 2 market-dependent petrol configuration with catalytic converter",
                "maxPowerStockInternals": "145-155 hp. The lower-output M111.940 profile still sits on the same fundamentally good 2.0-liter 16-valve base, so mild gains are possible through proper intake, exhaust, and calibration work. Even so, this is not an engine that transforms dramatically through tuning, and the detuned concept itself suggests that condition, smoothness, and correctness matter more than squeezing out headline figures. A healthy stock example usually feels better than a poorly tuned one. The sensible approach is always maintenance first, calibration second. This remains a durability-focused engine rather than a power-focused one.",
