    compression: "22.0:1",
    yearRange: "1993-1995",
    timingType: "Double-roller chain. The OM603 utilizes an incredibly robust, heavy-duty double-roller timing chain that essentially lasts the lifetime of the engine block. Unlike modern single-row chains, this unit rarely stretches. The only critical maintenance item is the hydraulic chain tensioner, which should be replaced preventatively around 250,000 km to ensure proper tension is maintained and the plastic chain guide rails do not fracture.",
    injectionType: "Indirect Injection (IDI) with a Bosch Mechanical Inline Injection Pump (M-Pump). This mechanical system is legendary for its durability and reliability. It is entirely devoid of complex electronics, relying on mechanical governors and vacuum lines. The injectors are inexpensive and robust, and the M-pump can often outlast the chassis itself, provided the fuel filters are changed regularly and water is kept out of the fuel system.",
    engineLifespan: "500 000 - 1 000 000+ km. The OM603 is widely considered one of the most durable internal combustion engines ever manufactured by Mercedes-Benz. The heavy cast-iron block and over-engineered forged internals are capable of astronomical mileages. However, this specific turbocharged variant (#14 cylinder head) is notorious for head gasket failures and cylinder head cracking if the engine is overheated even slightly. If the cooling system is maintained perfectly, the engine will run practically forever.",
    turboType: "Single KKK (Kühnle, Kopp & Kausch) or Garrett turbocharger. This is an old-school, robust journal-bearing turbocharger. It operates at relatively low boost pressures (around 0.8 to 0.9 bar) to provide a significant torque increase over the naturally aspirated variants. It lacks modern variable geometry, meaning it suffers from noticeable turbo lag, but its simplicity ensures long-term reliability provided the engine oil is kept clean.",
    ecuType: "Mechanical / Vacuum Controlled. The OM603.960 does not have an ECU in the modern sense. Fuel delivery is entirely managed by the mechanical Bosch injection pump, while idle speed and EGR (if equipped) are controlled via complex networks of vacuum lines and basic electrical relays. This makes the engine incredibly resilient to EMPs or electrical failures, but diagnosing running issues requires a deep understanding of pneumatic vacuum diagrams rather than plugging in an OBD scanner.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 1 / Pre-Euro",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods, crankshaft, and pistons are massive and capable of handling significantly more power. However, reaching these numbers requires installing larger turbochargers and modifying the mechanical Bosch injection pump (installing larger elements, often 7mm or 7.5mm), commonly referred to as a 'SuperTurboDiesel' build. The main limitation is the structural integrity of the aluminum cylinder head, which struggles to dissipate extreme heat.",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "9.5 - 11.0 L/100km",
      highway: "7.0 - 8.5 L/100km",
      mixed: "8.5 - 9.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "12.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "7.5L 10W-40 or 15W-40 High Zinc (ZDDP) Mineral or Semi-Synthetic. Because of the flat-tappet camshaft design and heavy mechanical loads, oils with high levels of Zinc and Phosphorus are absolutely vital to prevent rapid valvetrain wear. Modern low-ash oils designed for DPFs will destroy this engine.",
    commonIssues: [
      "Cylinder Head Cracking (#14 Head): The most infamous issue with the turbocharged OM603. The aluminum cylinder head (casting number ending in 14) is highly susceptible to cracking between the valves or near the pre-combustion chambers due to thermal stress. A later, revised cylinder head (#17, #20, or #22) is the only permanent cure.",
      "Head Gasket Failure: The extreme cylinder pressures and thermal expansion differences between the iron block and aluminum head frequently cause the head gasket to fail, particularly around the rear cylinder (#6), allowing oil to mix with coolant.",
      "Vacuum System Leaks: The engine relies entirely on vacuum for transmission shifting (modulator pressure), EGR operation, engine shut-off, and HVAC controls. Brittle rubber connectors lead to vacuum leaks, resulting in harsh transmission shifts, failure to shut off with the key, and erratic idle.",
      "Viscous Fan Clutch Failure: The engine cooling fan relies on a viscous clutch that degrades with age. When it fails to engage at high temperatures, the engine will overheat rapidly in traffic, which is absolutely fatal to the fragile #14 cylinder head.",
      "Injection Pump Delivery Valve Leaks: The O-rings sealing the delivery valves on top of the Bosch injection pump dry out and shrink, causing diesel fuel to leak visibly down the side of the pump and creating a strong fuel odor in the cabin."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Overheating Damage: The OM603 is completely unforgiving of overheating. Even a brief spike in temperature caused by a failed thermostat, ruptured hose, or dead fan clutch will warp the cylinder head and blow the head gasket, resulting in a massively expensive repair.",
      "Runaway Engine Risk: If the turbocharger seals fail completely, the engine can begin consuming its own engine oil as fuel. Because it is mechanically injected, turning off the key will not stop the engine. It will rev uncontrollably until it destroys itself unless the air intake is physically choked off.",
      "Transmission Vacuum Modulator Failure: The 722.3 automatic transmission shifts based on vacuum signals from the engine. If the vacuum lines crack or the VCV (Vacuum Control Valve) on the injection pump fails, the transmission will shift violently, eventually destroying the internal clutch packs.",
      "Glow Plug Snapping: The long, thin glow plugs can become carbon-welded into the cylinder head. If excessive force is used during removal, the plug will snap off in the head, requiring the cylinder head to be removed for professional extraction.",
      "Corrosion (W124 Chassis): While the engine is robust, the W124 chassis is highly prone to structural rust in the rear subframe mounts, jacking points, and front spring perches. Severe rust can render the vehicle unsafe and uneconomical to repair."
    ],
