    "fuelType": "Petrol",
    "cylinders": 6,
    "valves": 24,
    "compression": "10.0:1",
    "yearRange": "1993-1995",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-six timing layout. The M104.992 is the definitive normal-production petrol flagship for the facelift W124 range, giving the E 320 the kind of creamy, expensive-feeling straight-six character that makes the best six-cylinder 124s feel a class above the four-cylinder cars. It combines genuine smoothness with a noticeably stronger mid-range than the 2.8-liter versions, so the car feels effortless rather than urgent. In a healthy example, the engine pulls with quiet authority, revs cleanly, and delivers the kind of polished long-distance refinement that explains why the E 320 remains such a respected all-rounder. In a neglected example, however, the car can still feel acceptable while quietly losing the sharpness, smoothness, and richness that should define it.",
    "injectionType": "HFM-SFI electronically controlled multi-point fuel injection with hot-film air-mass measurement. This is a more modern and more adaptive control philosophy than the earlier KE-based systems, so the engine is better at cold start behavior, part-throttle control, and general refinement when everything is working correctly. The flip side is that the engine depends heavily on accurate air-mass readings, proper intake sealing, healthy engine wiring, and strong ignition quality. When the baseline is right, the engine feels smooth, intelligent, and properly premium. When it is not, the car often still runs well enough to hide its faults, which is exactly why mediocre examples get tolerated for too long.",
    "engineLifespan": "320,000 to 500,000 km. The M104.992 is fundamentally durable, and the block, crankshaft, and bottom end are not usually what make ownership painful. What determines long-term quality is whether the car has been protected from the usual M104 ageing pattern: head-gasket seepage, tired cooling hardware, degraded intake and vacuum rubber, ignition weakness, wiring deterioration, and years of lazy diagnosis. A genuinely cared-for E 320 can still feel superb at very high mileage. A neglected one often drives like a collection of medium faults that have not yet become one catastrophic failure.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated inline-six petrol engine. The absence of turbocharging is a central part of the appeal because the power delivery is linear, cultured, and mechanically honest. The engine does not hit hard; it builds speed in a rich and effortless way that suits the W124 chassis perfectly. This gives the E 320 a mature, expensive-feeling character rather than a dramatic one. A healthy car feels strong everywhere without ever feeling strained.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch HFM-SFI engine management with hot-film air-mass sensing, adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and electronic idle control. This makes the engine feel more polished than older Mercedes systems, but it also means a weak sensor, intake leak, poor ground, or bad replacement part can blur the entire character of the drivetrain. The system is not complex by modern standards, but it absolutely requires correct baseline diagnosis. A properly sorted HFM car feels coherent and precise. A half-sorted one feels disappointingly ordinary.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "235-250 hp. The M104.992 has modest naturally aspirated headroom, but the real gain almost always comes from restoring perfect mechanical and electronic baseline condition rather than chasing power. A sorted stock E 320 already feels strong, smooth, and more than fast enough for the chassis. Mild intake and exhaust work can sharpen it slightly, but they do not transform the engine's nature. This is a refinement-first powertrain, not a numbers platform.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "14.0-15.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "8.0-8.9 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.2-11.4 L/100km",
      "sporty": "15.5-18.0 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 7.0-7.5L 5W-40 or 10W-40 quality oil suitable for older Mercedes multivalve inline-six petrol engines. Conservative oil service matters more than stretched intervals because clean oil supports chain hardware, hydraulic lifters, sealing life, and overall engine cleanliness.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Head gasket seepage is the classic M104 ownership issue, usually beginning at the rear of the cylinder head and gradually turning from a minor leak into a larger cleanliness, heat, and maintenance problem.",
      "Cooling-system decline is a major risk because old radiators, weak water pumps, tired thermostats, ageing hoses, and stale coolant slowly reduce the thermal margin of the engine without always causing one dramatic overheating event.",
      "HFM drivability degradation is common on late M104 cars and often appears as slightly lazy throttle response, soft upper-range pull, uneven idle, or a general sense that the engine feels flatter than a healthy E 320 should.",
      "Vacuum leaks and intake-sealing faults are important because the engine depends on measured air quality and proper intake integrity to preserve smooth idle and clean part-throttle behavior.",
      "Ignition weakness from worn spark plugs, ageing leads, boots, or coils can make the engine feel coarse, slightly hesitant, or suspiciously ordinary.",
      "Upper-engine oil leaks around the valve-cover area are common and should not be normalized just because the car still performs well.",
      "Age-related biodegradable engine wiring on earlier M104-era cars can create intermittent faults, poor running, and time-consuming diagnosis if the harness condition has not been checked or renewed.",
      "Throttle-body and electronic throttle actuator age can create inconsistent response, idle irregularity, or limp-home behavior on cars with neglected electrical baselines.",
      "Engine mounts are frequently worn and can make the car feel harsher, busier, and less premium than the engine itself really is.",
      "Poor previous diagnosis is extremely common because the engine can remain usable while several small faults overlap and erode its character."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability of the M104.992 is that it stays driveable even when it is no longer healthy, so owners often tolerate a declining baseline for years.",
      "Head-gasket ageing remains a real ownership vulnerability because once oil seepage starts at the back of the head, the engine bay gets dirtier, hotter, and more difficult to assess honestly.",
      "Cooling complacency is dangerous because this engine hides thermal decline better than many owners expect, and that false calm is exactly what allows expensive neglect to build.",
      "HFM-system sensitivity creates a diagnostic vulnerability because the engine can be subtly wrong in several ways at once without one obvious single failure.",
      "Vacuum and intake integrity are especially vulnerable because small leaks degrade refinement long before they create obvious fault symptoms.",
      "Old wiring and connector condition are a major hidden vulnerability because intermittent electrical faults can mimic fueling or ignition problems and waste a lot of money in random parts replacement.",
      "Low-quality aftermarket ignition, sensor, and cooling parts are a major vulnerability because the engine is refined enough to expose them immediately.",
      "Oil seepage becomes a systems-level problem because once the engine bay stays oily, surrounding rubber, connectors, and service quality all deteriorate more quickly.",
      "Engine-mount neglect is a vulnerability because it distorts the owner's judgment of the engine itself; many cars feel rougher than they truly are because the mounting hardware is finished.",
      "Finally, this engine is vulnerable to optimistic sellers, because a merely decent M104.992 still feels smooth enough to disguise how much maintenance debt is sitting behind that impression."
    ],
