    "yearRange": "1995-2000",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. This fleet/export calibration keeps the same sturdy M111 mechanical base as the standard E 200, but with a noticeably softer delivery. It feels calmer, less eager in the mid-range, and more obviously tuned for durability, taxation logic, and low-drama use rather than normal-market performance. A healthy example should still feel smooth, honest, and mechanically tidy, but not especially quick.",
    "injectionType": "HFM electronically controlled multi-point fuel injection with hot-film air-mass measurement. In this detuned version, the system still delivers the same core strengths as the normal M111: clean starts, tidy part-throttle behavior, and predictable drivability when the intake, ignition, and wiring baseline is healthy. The lower-output calibration does not make the engine fundamentally simpler, so air-mass readings, vacuum integrity, throttle behavior, and wiring condition still matter.",
    "engineLifespan": "350,000 to 500,000 km. The M111.942 remains one of Mercedes' sturdier 1990s four-cylinder petrol engines, and the lower-output fleet tune does not change that basic reputation. What decides whether this engine feels dependable or disappointing is still the age of the surrounding systems: wiring, head-gasket condition, HFM intake integrity, ignition quality, cooling hardware, and previous repair standards. A well-kept example can cover high mileage with little drama, while a neglected one usually becomes tired through many medium faults rather than one catastrophic failure.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated inline-four petrol engine. The lack of turbocharging is part of the engine's appeal because the power delivery stays linear, simple, and mechanically transparent. In this detuned form, the engine feels even more relaxed than the standard E 200 and suits calm, low-stress driving better than any kind of enthusiastic use.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch HFM engine management with hot-film air-mass sensing, adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and electronic idle and throttle-related control. Even in a lower-output export calibration, the system still depends on sound intake sealing, correct sensor behavior, clean grounds, and healthy wiring. A sorted car feels neat and consistent, while a half-sorted one feels flat, vague, and older than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "145-155 hp. The lower-output fleet version is still based on the same durable M111 hardware, so real tuning headroom is mostly limited by the naturally aspirated layout rather than by bottom-end weakness. In practice, restoring the car to perfect baseline condition is far smarter than trying to modify it. This is a durability-first four-cylinder, not a power-first one.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "10.2-11.8 L/100km",
      "highway": "6.6-7.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.0-9.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "11.5-13.2 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 5.5-6.0L 5W-40 or 10W-40 quality oil suitable for older Mercedes petrol engines. Conservative oil changes remain wise because the M111 responds well to clean oil, especially in ageing HFM-era cars with mixed maintenance history.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Biodegradable engine wiring harness deterioration is one of the most important early M111-era problems. Once insulation hardens and cracks, the engine can develop false sensor readings, intermittent misfires, unstable idle, and confusing drivability faults.",
      "Head gasket seepage is common on ageing M111 engines, usually beginning at the front corner of the cylinder head where oil leakage gradually dirties the front of the engine and can be ignored for too long because the car still runs well.",
      "HFM air-mass meter drift can make the engine feel flatter, more hesitant, or slightly lazy, especially at part throttle, without always creating a dramatic failure.",
      "Throttle body and throttle-related wiring faults can create unstable idle, uneven tip-in response, or occasional limp-style behavior on neglected cars.",
      "Vacuum leaks in breather hoses, intake boots, and small rubber lines are common and quickly reduce idle quality, response sharpness, and overall refinement.",
      "Ignition weakness from worn plugs, leads, or ageing coil components can make the engine feel rougher and more sluggish than a healthy fleet-spec M111 should.",
      "Cooling-system ageing matters because old radiators, lazy thermostats, tired water pumps, and stale coolant slowly reduce reliability margin even on a relatively tough four-cylinder.",
      "Upper-engine oil leaks from the valve-cover area become common with age and should not be normalized just because the car remains driveable.",
      "Engine mounts often collapse with age and can make the car feel much rougher at idle than the engine itself really is.",
      "Poor previous diagnosis is common because the M111 usually stays usable while several small faults overlap and slowly erode its clean factory character."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability of the M111.942 fleet/export variant is that it stays driveable even when it is no longer truly healthy, so owners often postpone proper baseline work for years.",
      "Engine-harness condition is a major hidden vulnerability because degraded insulation can imitate sensor, fueling, or ignition faults and lead to expensive guesswork.",
      "Head-gasket seepage is a real ownership vulnerability because it often begins as a small oil leak that people tolerate until the whole front of the engine becomes dirty and harder to assess.",
      "HFM sensitivity creates a diagnostic vulnerability because the engine can be slightly wrong in several ways at once without one obvious major failure.",
      "Vacuum and breather-system integrity are especially vulnerable because small leaks noticeably reduce the neat and clean running character that defines a good M111.",
      "Cooling complacency is dangerous because this engine is durable enough to hide neglect for a long time, which encourages owners to postpone radiator, thermostat, and pump work.",
      "Low-quality aftermarket ignition, sensor, and cooling parts are a major vulnerability because the engine immediately feels less resolved when poor parts are fitted.",
      "Mount neglect is a hidden vulnerability because many owners blame the engine for roughness that is actually being transmitted by collapsed mounts.",
      "Old grounds and connector corrosion are a vulnerability because they can create annoying intermittent faults that feel bigger than they are.",
      "This engine is also vulnerable to being underestimated, which means service history is often worse than on more expensive W210 variants."
    ],
