    yearRange: "1996-1999",
    timingType: "Double-roller chain. The OM602 utilizes a phenomenally robust double-row timing chain architecture. This chain is heavily over-engineered for the engine's 129 hp output and is widely considered a lifetime component that rarely suffers from stretch. Preventative replacement of the hydraulic chain tensioner at 250,000 km is the only recommended maintenance to ensure proper tension is applied to the plastic guide rails, preventing them from fracturing from chain slap over extreme mileages.",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection (DI) with a Bosch VE Rotary Distributor Pump (VP37). This engine is entirely unique in the W210 lineup as it utilizes direct injection but pre-dates the Common-Rail (CDI) era. Fuel is injected directly into the combustion chamber by a mechanically driven rotary pump that features electronic actuation for precise timing and fueling. This architecture makes the engine incredibly fuel-efficient and exceptionally loud, producing a distinct, harsh 'tractor-like' clatter at idle. The top seals on the VP37 pump are notorious for leaking diesel fuel as they age, but resealing the pump is a very well-documented and affordable procedure.",
    engineLifespan: "500 000 - 1 000 000+ km. The OM602.982 is a legendary endurance motor. The cast-iron block and simple 10-valve aluminum cylinder head operate under very low thermal and mechanical stress. The direct injection system, while noisy, is far less prone to the catastrophic 'Black Death' issues of later CDI engines. Provided the cooling system is maintained to prevent cylinder head warping and the oil is changed regularly, this engine block will effortlessly outlast the W210 chassis.",
    turboType: "Single Garrett or KKK journal-bearing turbocharger with a traditional mechanical wastegate. The turbo is relatively small, providing excellent low-end response and a very usable 300 Nm of torque early in the rev range. It lacks complex variable geometry (VNT), ensuring long-term simplicity and reliability.",
    ecuType: "Bosch MSA 15. The ECU primarily manages the electronic actuator collar inside the Bosch VP37 rotary pump, controlling injection quantity and timing. Because this engine falls into the pre-facelift W210 era (1996-1999), it is highly vulnerable to the bio-degradable eco-wiring harness issue. The insulation on the engine wiring harness will inevitably turn to dust, causing catastrophic electrical shorts that can destroy the ECU and sensor inputs.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 2",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "200+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and pistons are highly robust. While the block can handle significant power increases, the VP37 rotary pump is the primary limiting factor for massive tuning. It cannot flow the volume of fuel required for 'SuperTurboDiesel' builds like the OM606 can, limiting its ultimate tuning potential to moderate Stage 1 or Stage 2 upgrades with larger injector nozzles.",
