    "yearRange": "2000-2002",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. The M111.957 is the facelift-era EVO version of the 2.0-liter Kompressor engine and feels more modern and more polished than the earlier supercharged M111.947. It gives the late W210 a smoother, cleaner, and more globally usable character, with better low-end flexibility and a more refined calibration. In healthy condition, it feels surprisingly strong for a 2.0-liter executive sedan.",
    "injectionType": "Electronically controlled multi-point fuel injection with updated ME engine management working together with mechanical supercharging. Compared with the earlier HFM-based Kompressor setup, the later EVO system feels cleaner in response, more consistent in traffic, and better integrated in part-throttle driving. It still depends heavily on correct air-path sealing, healthy sensors, good ignition quality, and stable throttle-body behavior.",
    "engineLifespan": "300,000 to 450,000 km. The M111.957 remains fundamentally a durable Mercedes four-cylinder, and the core long block is usually not the expensive part of ownership. Real-world cost comes from the age and condition of the surrounding systems: intake and vacuum integrity, throttle body behavior, ignition quality, supercharger drive hardware, cooling-system health, engine mounts, and the quality of previous repairs. A properly sorted example can remain smooth, quick enough, and genuinely satisfying for a very long time.",
    "turboType": "Mechanically supercharged inline-four petrol engine using a belt-driven compressor rather than an exhaust-driven turbocharger. That gives it an immediate and linear response that suits the W210 well, especially in everyday driving where low-end and mid-range torque matter more than top-end drama. In EVO form, the delivery feels tidier and more mature than the earlier Kompressor setup. A healthy example feels eager without feeling crude.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes ME engine management with electronic throttle control, adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and supercharged calibration logic. This later control system makes the engine feel more modern than the earlier HFM cars, but it also means throttle adaptation, electrical baseline quality, and sensor accuracy matter more than many owners expect. A sorted car feels smooth and precise. A neglected one feels inconsistent and older than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "195-210 hp. The M111.957 has some modest headroom on stock internals, but the sensible path is still to perfect the engine's baseline condition rather than chase modifications. A healthy stock example already delivers the right balance for a W210: enough torque, enough smoothness, and enough pace for real road use. Most disappointing tuned examples are simply badly maintained standard engines in disguise.",
