    "timingType": "Single overhead camshaft per bank, 3-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 90-degree V6 timing layout. The M112.914 is the facelift-era enlarged E 240 engine and it gives the W210 a slightly fuller and more effortless character than the earlier 2.4-liter version. It is still a refinement-led V6 rather than a performance engine, but the extra displacement helps the car feel calmer and less strained in everyday use. In healthy condition, it feels smooth, quiet, and properly premium.",
    "injectionType": "Electronically controlled manifold injection with closed-loop catalytic control. The M112 family feels more modern and more refined than the older M111 fours, and the facelift E 240's 2.6-liter V6 adds a little more torque and flexibility without changing the car's core personality. The system is not especially fragile, but it still depends on good intake sealing, healthy sensors, strong ignition quality, and stable electrical condition to remain properly smooth.",
    "engineLifespan": "300,000 to 450,000 km. The M112.914 is fundamentally a durable V6 and the basic engine architecture is one of the more successful Mercedes petrol designs of its era. What usually shapes ownership cost is not the block itself but the age of surrounding systems: crankcase ventilation, intake sealing, ignition components, cooling system condition, oil leaks, engine mounts, and previous service quality. A properly maintained example can remain very smooth and dependable for high mileage.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. The lack of forced induction suits the character of the facelift E 240 because delivery is smooth, predictable, and more refined than exciting. It does not feel especially fast by modern standards, but it does feel relaxed and expensive in the way it builds speed. A healthy M112.914 is about flexibility and refinement first.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz electronic engine management with manifold injection, adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and emission control logic. This gives the M112.914 polished day-to-day manners, but small faults in air leaks, ignition quality, crankcase ventilation, or sensor baseline can still dull the whole drivetrain. A sorted car feels creamy and cohesive. A neglected one feels softer, rougher, and older than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "185-195 hp. The M112.914 is not a platform that rewards serious naturally aspirated tuning, and the sensible approach is still to restore the engine to perfect baseline condition rather than chase modifications. A healthy stock facelift E 240 already delivers the character Mercedes intended. The real value here is smoothness and torque delivery, not headline tuning gains.",
