    timingType: "Double-roller chain. The M119 utilizes an incredibly robust double-row timing chain that is virtually immune to stretching under normal use. However, the upper timing chain guide rails are constructed from plastic that becomes dangerously brittle from decades of extreme V8 heat cycles. If these guide rails fracture, the broken plastic debris will drop into the timing cover and jam the chain sprocket, causing immediate, catastrophic valve-to-piston contact. Preventative replacement of these upper plastic guides every 150,000 km is absolutely mandatory to save the irreplaceable 6.0L block.",
    injectionType: "Multi-Point Sequential Fuel Injection via Bosch ME-SFI (Motor Electronics). Unlike the older W124 E 60 AMG which used LH-Jetronic, the W210 generation transitioned to the newer ME system, eliminating the twin distributors in favor of a coil-pack ignition system. However, the very early W210 models still suffered from the infamous bio-degradable eco-wiring harness issue. The insulation on the engine harness will crumble to dust, causing severe electrical shorts that can destroy the electronic throttle body and the engine control module.",
    engineLifespan: "250 000 - 400 000+ km. The M119 engine block is legendary for its endurance, featuring a closed-deck aluminum block with Alusil cylinder coatings. The 6.0L AMG variant features unique, highly stressed forged internals that are significantly more expensive and difficult to source than the standard 5.0L. While the physical block is nearly indestructible, the engine's lifespan is entirely dictated by the owner's willingness to combat severe oil leaks, replace the crumbling eco-wiring harness, and strictly maintain the cooling system to prevent head gasket failures.",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated. The M119 in the E 60 AMG relies entirely on its massive 6.0-liter displacement and advanced 32-valve, four-camshaft breathing capability to generate immense, immediate torque. The throttle response is razor-sharp and completely linear, delivering a pure, unfiltered V8 power delivery that forced-induction engines simply cannot replicate.",
    ecuType: "Bosch ME-SFI. The E 60 AMG utilizes specific AMG-tuned control modules that map the fueling and ignition timing specifically for the 6.0L displacement. These modules are incredibly rare and astronomically expensive to replace if they are short-circuited by a crumbling eco-wiring harness. Aftermarket tuning is practically nonexistent and highly discouraged, as the factory AMG mapping is already perfectly optimized for the engine's hardware.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 2",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "450+ hp. The AMG-specific forged pistons, heavy-duty connecting rods, and internally balanced crankshaft are over-engineered to extreme degrees. While the block can technically handle forced induction, attempting to turbocharge or supercharge an original, numbers-matching E 60 AMG is considered sacrilege in the collector community and destroys its immense historical and financial value.",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "18.0 - 22.0 L/100km",
      highway: "11.0 - 13.5 L/100km",
      mixed: "14.5 - 17.0 L/100km",
      sporty: "25.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "8.0L 10W-40 or 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.3 or 229.5). High-quality synthetic oil with high zinc content is vital to protect the four camshafts and 32 hydraulic lifters from aggressive wear under high-RPM loads.",
    commonIssues: [
      "Bio-degradable Wiring Harness Failure: The eco-friendly wiring insulation crumbles to dust from engine heat on early models. This causes the wires to touch, shorting out the throttle body, mass airflow sensor, and the primary ME control module.",
      "Timing Chain Guide Rail Fracture: The upper plastic guide rails turn brittle and snap, falling into the timing chain path and instantly destroying the engine.",
      "Camshaft Oiler Tube Leaks: The plastic oiler tubes that lubricate the camshaft lobes crack and blow off. This starves the camshafts of oil, causing rapid lobe wear and a loud ticking noise from the top of the engine.",
      "Extensive Oil Leaks: The M119 is notorious for weeping oil from the valve cover gaskets, the front timing cover U-seal, and the camshaft adjuster solenoids.",
      "W210 Spring Perch Rust: The front spring perches on the W210 chassis rust heavily and can completely tear away from the body, causing the front suspension to instantly collapse."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Irreplaceable Parts Scarcity: Engine internals specific to the 6.0L AMG variant (pistons, crankshaft, ECU) are virtually impossible to find new. A catastrophic engine failure often means sourcing custom-machined parts or losing the car's originality.",
      "ECU Destruction via Harness Short: If the eco-wiring harness shorts out, it sends a power surge directly into the computer, frying the motherboard. Replacing a genuine E 60 AMG control module costs thousands, if one can even be located.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The 5-speed 722.6 automatic transmission is robust, but managing 580 Nm of torque will eventually burn out the clutch packs if the fluid is not changed religiously.",
      "Valve-to-Piston Contact (Guides): Ignoring the ticking sound of a loose timing chain guarantees that the plastic guides will shatter and the chain will jump, requiring a complete engine rebuild.",
      "Lethal Chassis Corrosion: The engine is a masterpiece, but the W210 chassis is highly susceptible to structural rust in the spring perches, subframes, and jacking points, rendering the vehicle completely unsafe."
    ],
