    compression: "22.0:1",
    yearRange: "1995-1998",
    timingType: "Double-roller chain. The OM604 uses a highly durable duplex timing chain, carrying over the legendary reliability of the older OM601 but driving twin camshafts. This chain essentially never stretches under the meager 88 hp output. The hydraulic chain tensioner is the only preventative maintenance item, recommended for replacement around 250,000 km to prevent cold-start chain slap against the plastic guide rails.",
    injectionType: "Indirect Injection (IDI) via pre-combustion chambers. Unlike the legendary inline Bosch pumps of its predecessors, the OM604 utilizes a Lucas EPIC (Electronically Programmed Injection Control) rotary distributor pump. This pump is the absolute Achilles' heel of the engine. The internal rotor head seals fail constantly, leaking diesel everywhere, and the electronic metering sensors are highly sensitive to heat and poor fuel. Rebuilding the Lucas pump is incredibly expensive, and many owners opt to retrofit an older mechanical Bosch inline pump from an OM601 to achieve true reliability.",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The cast-iron block and 16-valve aluminum cylinder head are physically indestructible. The engine block will easily outlast the car. However, the lifespan is heavily dictated by the Lucas EPIC injection pump. When this pump inevitably fails, the repair cost frequently exceeds the value of the aging W210 chassis, leading many of these cars to be scrapped despite having perfectly healthy engine blocks.",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated. To compensate for the lack of forced induction, the OM604 uses a complex resonance intake manifold. Vacuum-actuated flaps dynamically alter airflow routing based on RPM, providing a noticeable bump in mid-range torque that makes the car feel slightly punchier in city driving than the older 8-valve diesels.",
    ecuType: "Lucas EDC (Electronic Diesel Control). This module exclusively manages the complex electronic actuators inside the Lucas EPIC injection pump. Because this is an early W210, it is heavily plagued by the bio-degradable eco-wiring harness issue. The wiring insulation crumbles to dust, causing electrical shorts that fry the Lucas pump sensors and the EDC module.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 2",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "150+ hp. The forged internals are massively over-engineered. However, the Lucas injection pump cannot flow enough fuel to support tuning. The only way to increase power significantly is to rip out the Lucas pump entirely, install a mechanical Bosch M-Pump with larger elements, and bolt on a turbocharger. The block handles this effortlessly.",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "8.0 - 9.0 L/100km",
      highway: "5.5 - 6.5 L/100km",
      mixed: "6.5 - 7.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "8.5+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "6.0L 10W-40 or 15W-40 High Zinc (ZDDP) Mineral or Semi-Synthetic. High-zinc oil is absolutely mandatory to protect the flat-tappet camshafts from aggressive wear. Modern low-ash synthetic oils will severely damage the valvetrain.",
    commonIssues: [
      "Lucas EPIC Injection Pump Failure: The quintessential OM604 flaw. The internal seals leak profusely, and the electronic sensors fail, causing hard starting, Limp Mode, and catastrophic fuel leaks.",
      "Bio-degradable Wiring Harness Failure: The eco-friendly wiring insulation crumbles to dust from engine heat. This causes the wires to touch, shorting out sensors and potentially frying the Lucas EDC module.",
      "Glow Plug Snapping: The long glow plugs needed to reach through the 16-valve head frequently seize. Snapping one during removal requires pulling the cylinder head.",
      "Resonance Intake Manifold Flap Seizing: The vacuum-actuated flaps inside the complex variable intake manifold can seize up due to a combination of carbon soot from the EGR valve and oil vapor.",
      "W210 Spring Perch Rust: The front spring perches on the W210 chassis rust heavily and can completely tear away from the body, causing the front suspension to instantly collapse."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Financial Ruin via Lucas Pump: Rebuilding or replacing the Lucas EPIC injection pump often costs more than the car is worth, making it the primary reason these engines are scrapped.",
      "Dangerous Lack of Highway Speed: With only 88 horsepower pushing a heavy W210, acceleration is dangerously slow by modern standards. Merging onto fast-moving highways requires intense pre-planning.",
      "ECU Destruction via Harness Short: If the degrading wiring harness shorts out, replacing the EDC module and the harness itself is an incredibly expensive repair.",
      "Transmission Vacuum Modulator Failure: The 722.4 automatic transmission determines shift timing entirely via engine vacuum. If a line cracks, the transmission shifts violently, eventually destroying the internal clutch packs.",
      "Lethal Chassis Corrosion: The engine block will outlive the car. The W210 chassis is highly susceptible to structural rust in the spring perches, subframes, and jacking points, rendering the vehicle completely unsafe."
    ],
