    yearRange: "1995-1998",
    timingType: "Double-roller chain. The OM604 uses a highly durable duplex timing chain. Because this specific fleet version is brutally restricted to 82 hp, the mechanical stress on the chain is practically non-existent. It is a true lifetime component that will never stretch under normal use. The hydraulic chain tensioner is the only preventative maintenance item, recommended for replacement around 300,000 km to ensure the plastic guide rails do not fracture from age.",
    injectionType: "Indirect Injection (IDI) via pre-combustion chambers. This heavily detuned variant still utilizes the disastrous Lucas EPIC (Electronically Programmed Injection Control) rotary distributor pump. The pump's internal rotor head seals fail constantly, leaking diesel everywhere, and the electronic metering sensors are highly sensitive to heat and poor fuel. The 82 hp restriction was implemented via software in the Lucas EDC module to maximize fleet longevity and meet strict taxation brackets, but the detune does not improve the atrocious reliability of the Lucas pump itself.",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 500 000+ km. Because it produces an astoundingly low 82 horsepower, the cast-iron block and 16-valve aluminum cylinder head operate in a state of perpetual mechanical slumber. The engine block will effortlessly outlast the car. However, the lifespan is heavily dictated by the Lucas EPIC injection pump. When this pump inevitably fails, the repair cost frequently exceeds the value of the aging W210 chassis, leading many of these cars to be scrapped.",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated. To compensate for the severe lack of power, the OM604 uses a complex resonance intake manifold. Vacuum-actuated flaps dynamically alter airflow routing based on RPM, providing the only usable mid-range torque the engine can muster. If these flaps seize, the car becomes dangerously slow.",
    ecuType: "Lucas EDC (Electronic Diesel Control). This module manages the complex electronic actuators inside the Lucas EPIC injection pump and imposes the severe 82 hp fleet restriction. Because this is an early W210, it is heavily plagued by the bio-degradable eco-wiring harness issue. The wiring insulation crumbles to dust, causing electrical shorts that fry the Lucas pump sensors and the EDC module.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 2",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "150+ hp. The forged internals are massively over-engineered. However, the Lucas injection pump cannot flow enough fuel to support tuning, and the EDC module is highly restrictive. The only way to increase power significantly is to rip out the Lucas pump entirely, install a mechanical Bosch M-Pump, and bolt on a turbocharger.",
