    "yearRange": "1997-2002",
    "timingType": "Single overhead camshaft per bank, 3-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 90-degree V6 timing layout. The M112.921 is the core W210 E 280 V6 and it gives the car a noticeably more effortless and complete character than the smaller E 240 variants. It is not an aggressive performance engine, but it suits the E-Class extremely well because it delivers smooth torque, quiet running, and a more expensive feel in normal use. In healthy condition, it feels creamy, flexible, and properly premium.",
    "injectionType": "Electronically controlled manifold injection with closed-loop catalytic control. The M112 family feels more modern and more refined than the older M104 and M111-era drivetrains in some everyday respects, and the 2.8-liter version gives the W210 a stronger and more relaxed mid-range without becoming complicated in the forced-induction sense. The system is not especially fragile, but it still depends on good intake sealing, healthy sensors, strong ignition quality, and stable electrical condition to remain properly smooth.",
    "engineLifespan": "320,000 to 450,000 km. The M112.921 is fundamentally a durable V6 and the basic engine architecture is one of the more successful Mercedes petrol designs of its era. What usually shapes ownership cost is not the block itself but the age of surrounding systems: crankcase ventilation, intake sealing, ignition components, cooling system condition, oil leaks, engine mounts, transmission condition, and previous service quality. A properly maintained example can remain very smooth and dependable for high mileage.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. The lack of forced induction suits the E 280 because delivery is smooth, predictable, and more refined than dramatic. It does not feel brutally fast by modern standards, but it does feel stronger and more effortless than the smaller W210 petrol engines, especially in mid-range road use. A healthy M112.921 is about smooth torque and refinement first.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz electronic engine management with manifold injection, adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and emission control logic. This gives the M112.921 polished day-to-day manners, but small faults in air leaks, ignition quality, crankcase ventilation, or sensor baseline can still dull the whole drivetrain. A sorted car feels creamy and cohesive. A neglected one feels softer, rougher, and older than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220-235 hp. The M112.921 has some naturally aspirated headroom on stock internals, but the sensible ownership path is still to restore perfect baseline condition rather than chase tuning gains. A healthy stock E 280 already delivers the character Mercedes intended. The real value here is refinement, torque delivery, and balance rather than headline tuning numbers.",
