    "yearRange": "1997-2002",
    "timingType": "Single overhead camshaft per bank, 3-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 90-degree V6 timing layout. This version uses the same basic M112.921 2.8-liter V6 architecture as the regular E 280, but with a softer regional calibration intended for lower-octane fuel tolerance and more conservative operating behavior. It feels slightly less eager at the top end than the full-output version, but it still delivers the smooth and refined character expected from a W210 V6. In healthy condition, it feels creamy, flexible, and relaxed rather than aggressively strong.",
    "injectionType": "Electronically controlled manifold injection with closed-loop catalytic control. The core system remains the same M112 layout, but in this market-specific calibration the fueling and ignition strategy are tuned more conservatively to prioritize fuel quality tolerance, consistency, and drivability over maximum output. The system is still not especially fragile, but it depends on good intake sealing, healthy sensors, strong ignition quality, and stable electrical condition to remain properly smooth.",
    "engineLifespan": "320,000 to 450,000 km. The M112.921 remains fundamentally a durable V6 in this softer regional form, and the slightly reduced output does not change the basic long-term strength of the engine. What usually shapes ownership cost is not the block itself but the age of surrounding systems: crankcase ventilation, intake sealing, ignition components, cooling system condition, oil leaks, engine mounts, transmission condition, and previous service quality. A properly maintained example can remain very smooth and dependable for high mileage.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. The lack of forced induction suits this regional E 280 version because delivery is smooth, predictable, and refinement-led rather than dramatic. Compared with the regular 204 hp version, it feels a touch calmer and slightly less urgent in the upper range, but still more than adequate for normal executive-car use. A healthy example is about smooth torque and quiet confidence first.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz electronic engine management with manifold injection, adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and emission control logic. In this lower-output regional tune, the calibration emphasizes knock safety, smoothness, and compatibility with less consistent fuel quality rather than stronger headline figures. A sorted car feels creamy and cohesive. A neglected one feels softer, rougher, and older than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220-230 hp. The mechanical base remains the same strong M112.921 V6, so this softer calibration still has modest headroom relative to its market tune. In practice, restoring the engine to perfect baseline condition is still far more important than modifying it. The real value here is refinement, tolerance, and durability rather than chasing power.",
