    compression: "10.0:1",
    yearRange: "1997-2002",
    timingType: "Double-roller chain. The M113 engine features a remarkably tough double-row timing chain that is famous for outlasting the vehicle itself. Unlike its M119 predecessor, the M113 is not known for catastrophic plastic guide rail failures, though high-mileage examples should be inspected for general chain slack. A slight rattle on cold start may indicate a failing hydraulic tensioner, which should be replaced preventatively.",
    injectionType: "Multi-Point Sequential Fuel Injection (ME-SFI). The Bosch ME system is highly advanced, extremely reliable, and provides perfect fueling mapping. Crucially, the M113 completely escapes the devastating bio-degradable eco-wiring harness catastrophe of the mid-1990s. The fuel injectors are standard electronic units that rarely fail, provided the fuel filter is changed regularly.",
    engineLifespan: "400 000 - 800 000+ km. The M113 is widely considered one of the most reliable and bulletproof V8 engines ever built by Mercedes-Benz. The aluminum block utilizes incredibly tough Silitec (Alusil) cylinder linings that show practically zero wear. By abandoning the complex 32-valve setup for a simpler 24-valve (3 valves per cylinder) design, engineers dramatically reduced mechanical complexity and increased the engine's lifespan.",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated. The M113 relies entirely on its 4.3-liter displacement to generate a smooth wave of 400 Nm of torque. This provides a muscular, instant throttle response that pins the driver to the seat from incredibly low RPMs, without the lag or thermal complexity of a turbocharger.",
    ecuType: "Bosch ME-SFI 2.0 / 2.8. The ME system manages the fueling, ignition, and electronic throttle perfectly. The engine utilizes a twin-spark ignition system (two spark plugs per cylinder) to maximize combustion efficiency and reduce emissions. The MAF (Mass Airflow) sensor is a common failure point that will cause hesitation when contaminated.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 3",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "500+ hp. The forged internals of the M113 engine are legendary for their strength. Bolting an aftermarket supercharger (like a Kleemann kit) to a naturally aspirated E 430 safely pushes it past 400 horsepower without opening the block.",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "14.5 - 17.0 L/100km",
      highway: "8.5 - 10.5 L/100km",
      mixed: "11.0 - 12.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "16.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "8.0L 5W-40 or 0W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5). High-quality synthetic oil is absolutely vital to protect the valvetrain and keep the Silitec cylinder linings in perfect condition.",
    commonIssues: [
      "Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS) Failure: The single most common issue on the M113. The sensor suffers from heat soak and fails, causing the engine to suddenly stall while driving or refuse to restart until cooled down.",
      "Valve Cover & Breather Leaks: The M113 notoriously leaks oil from the valve cover gaskets and the upper breather boxes. The breather box sealant degrades over time, weeping oil directly onto the hot exhaust manifolds.",
      "Harmonic Balancer Separation: The rubber isolator in the main crankshaft pulley degrades. If ignored, the outer ring separates and can machine a hole directly into the timing cover.",
      "Mass Airflow Sensor (MAF) Contamination: A dirty MAF will cause poor idle, hesitation, and a significant loss of power without always triggering a Check Engine Light.",
      "Rear Main Seal Weeping: Over time, the rear main seal will begin to weep synthetic oil down the back of the engine block."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Lethal Chassis Corrosion (W210): The M113 engine is immortal, but it is bolted into a highly vulnerable chassis. Severe structural rust in the front spring perches, subframes, and jacking points will render the car unsafe to drive.",
      "722.6 Conductor Plate Failure: The 5-speed automatic transmission will drop into Limp Mode and refuse to shift if the internal conductor plate fails due to neglected 'lifetime' fluid changes.",
      "Twin-Spark Ignition Costs: Servicing the ignition system requires 16 spark plugs and 8 individual coil packs, making routine tune-ups surprisingly expensive.",
      "W210 Spring Perch Collapse: The infamous front spring perches will violently tear away from the rusted chassis if not proactively welded and reinforced.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: While the 722.6 is strong, ignoring fluid changes while deploying 400 Nm of V8 torque will eventually wear out the torque converter."
    ],
