    "compression": "10.0:1",
    "yearRange": "1995-1997",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-six timing layout. The M104.995 gives the early W210 E 320 the classic Mercedes straight-six character in one of its richest naturally aspirated forms. It feels smoother, stronger, and more effortless than the smaller M104 and M112 entry engines, and it suits the chassis with a properly old-school executive-car feel. In healthy condition, it feels creamy, quiet, and confidently strong in normal road use.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch HFM Motronic-controlled multi-point fuel injection with hot-film air-mass measurement. This system gives the M104.995 clean starts, strong part-throttle manners, and the polished throttle response expected from a healthy mid-1990s Mercedes inline-six. It is not especially exotic by modern standards, but it still depends on correct airflow measurement, vacuum integrity, sound wiring, and stable ignition quality to feel right.",
    "engineLifespan": "350,000 to 500,000 km. The M104.995 is fundamentally a durable Mercedes straight-six and the long block itself is usually not the reason ownership becomes expensive. Real-world cost comes from the age and condition of the surrounding systems: engine wiring, head-gasket behavior, HFM intake baseline, ignition quality, cooling-system condition, throttle actuator health, engine mounts, and the standard of previous repairs. A genuinely maintained example can remain smooth and satisfying for very high mileage.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated inline-six petrol engine. The absence of forced induction suits the early E 320 perfectly because delivery is linear, progressive, and refined instead of dramatic. It does not feel explosive in a modern turbo sense, but it does feel rich, elastic, and expensive in the way it builds speed. A healthy M104.995 is about smooth torque and mechanical grace rather than sudden shove.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch HFM Motronic engine management with adaptive fueling, ignition coordination, and hot-film air-mass sensing. This gives the M104.995 the polished drivability expected of a good Mercedes straight-six, but small faults in intake sealing, grounding, wiring, throttle control, or ignition quality can noticeably dull the whole experience. A sorted car feels smooth and cohesive. A tired one feels slightly flat, inconsistent, and older than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-dependent catalytic petrol configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "235-250 hp. The M104.995 has modest naturally aspirated headroom on stock internals, but the sensible ownership path is still to restore perfect baseline condition rather than chase tuning gains. A healthy stock E 320 already delivers the character this engine is supposed to have. It rewards maintenance far more than modification.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "14.5-16.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.8-8.8 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.2-11.3 L/100km",
      "sporty": "15.5-18.0 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 7.5-8.0L 5W-40 fully synthetic oil suitable for older Mercedes inline-six petrol engines. Conservative oil changes remain wise because the M104 responds well to clean oil and generally ages best when service intervals are kept sensible.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Biodegradable engine wiring harness deterioration remains one of the most important M104-era problems. Once insulation hardens and cracks, the engine can develop false sensor readings, intermittent misfires, unstable idle, and confusing drivability faults.",
      "Head gasket oil seepage is a classic M104 issue and often begins externally before the engine shows any major running problem.",
      "Electronic throttle actuator and throttle-related wiring faults can create unstable idle, hesitant response, limp-home behavior, or inconsistent part-throttle operation.",
      "HFM air-mass meter drift can make the engine feel flatter, less precise, and less silky than a healthy M104 should.",
      "Vacuum leaks in breather hoses, intake boots, and small rubber lines are common and quickly reduce idle quality and throttle smoothness.",
      "Ignition weakness from worn plugs, coil-related faults, or poor-quality replacement parts can make the straight-six feel rougher than it should.",
      "Cooling-system ageing matters because old radiators, thermostats, water pumps, and stale coolant slowly reduce reliability margin.",
      "Upper-engine oil leaks from valve cover and related sealing areas become common with age and should not be normalized.",
      "Engine mounts often collapse with age and can make the drivetrain feel much rougher at idle than the engine itself really is.",
      "Connector corrosion and weak grounds can create frustrating intermittent faults that waste diagnosis time."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability of the M104.995 is that it stays pleasant to drive even when it is no longer truly healthy, so owners often postpone proper baseline work.",
      "Engine-harness condition is a major hidden vulnerability because degraded insulation can imitate sensor, fueling, ignition, or throttle faults and lead to expensive guesswork.",
      "Head-gasket seepage is a real vulnerability because it often begins as a manageable oil leak that owners normalize for too long.",
      "Throttle actuator age is a serious drivability vulnerability because it can make an otherwise strong engine feel inconsistent or unreliable.",
      "HFM sensitivity creates a diagnostic vulnerability because the engine can lose much of its silkiness without one dramatic failure.",
      "Vacuum and breather-system integrity remain vulnerable because small leaks noticeably reduce the clean and quiet running character that defines a good M104.",
      "Cooling complacency is dangerous because the engine is durable enough to hide neglect for a long time before it finally becomes expensive.",
      "Low-quality aftermarket ignition, sensor, and cooling parts are a major vulnerability because the engine immediately feels less resolved when poor parts are fitted.",
      "Mount neglect is a hidden vulnerability because many owners blame the engine for roughness that is actually caused by collapsed mounts.",
      "This engine is also vulnerable to being underestimated, which means service history is often worse than the smooth driving feel initially suggests."
    ],
