    "yearRange": "2002-2006",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven supercharged inline-four timing layout. The M271.946 is the core pre-facelift E 200 Kompressor engine and it gives the W211 a more energetic low-end feel than older naturally aspirated Mercedes four-cylinders. It is not a luxury-smooth six-cylinder engine, but it suits everyday E-Class use better than many people expect when healthy. In good condition, it feels responsive, reasonably refined, and stronger in the mid-range than the displacement suggests.",
    "injectionType": "Electronically controlled sequential multi-point petrol injection with mechanically driven supercharger boost management. The M271 Kompressor family feels more eager at low and medium engine speeds than older non-supercharged Mercedes four-cylinders, and this 1.8-liter version gives the W211 acceptable everyday flexibility without moving into six-cylinder running costs. The system depends on healthy ignition parts, good intake sealing, correct air measurement, and stable supercharger operation to feel right.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 350,000 km. The M271.946 can be a durable engine when serviced properly, but it is more sensitive to oil quality, timing-chain condition, cooling-system health, and ignition quality than older low-stressed naturally aspirated Mercedes petrol engines. Real ownership cost usually comes from layered wear in surrounding systems rather than one guaranteed catastrophic failure point. A well-maintained example can remain dependable and pleasant for high mileage.",
    "turboType": "Mechanically supercharged petrol engine with strong low-end and mid-range response for its size. The Kompressor setup suits the E 200 because it helps the heavy W211 feel more willing in normal driving than a small naturally aspirated engine would. It does not feel especially fast at high speeds, but it feels decently responsive in day-to-day use. A healthy M271.946 is about usable torque and clean boosted response rather than outright excitement.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz electronic engine management with adaptive fueling, ignition control, knock regulation, and supercharger-related load coordination. This gives the M271.946 polished daily drivability, but small faults in timing adaptation, airflow measurement, ignition quality, crankcase ventilation, or intake sealing can noticeably reduce smoothness and response. A sorted car feels coherent and reasonably refined. A neglected one feels rougher, flatter, and more strained than it should.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 4 / market-dependent early Euro 3 configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "190-205 hp. The M271.946 has modest headroom on stock internals through sensible supercharger-related tuning and supporting-condition improvements, but the sensible ownership path is still to restore perfect baseline condition rather than chase power. A healthy stock E 200 Kompressor already delivers the character this version was designed for. It rewards maintenance and careful diagnostics more than aggressive modification.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "11.0-12.8 L/100km",
      "highway": "6.2-7.2 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.0-9.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "11.8-14.2 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 5.5L 5W-40 or 0W-40 fully synthetic oil meeting suitable Mercedes-Benz petrol-engine approvals. Conservative oil changes are strongly recommended because the M271 responds best to clean oil, especially in relation to timing-chain health, supercharger-side reliability, and long-term smoothness.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing-chain stretch, chain noise, or tensioner-related wear can become a serious ownership topic if oil changes have been neglected or stretched too far.",
      "Camshaft sprocket and timing-related wear can create rough running, adaptation faults, startup noise, or warning-light behavior on tired engines.",
      "Crankcase ventilation and breather-related faults can cause oil consumption, unstable idle behavior, and intake contamination over time.",
      "Mass-airflow and intake sealing issues can make the engine feel softer, rougher, and less linear than a healthy Kompressor engine should.",
      "Ignition weakness from worn spark plugs or ageing coil packs can quickly produce misfires, rough running, and poor boosted drivability.",
      "Supercharger belt, pulley, or related drive-system wear can reduce consistency and refinement if left ignored.",
      "Cooling-system ageing matters because old thermostats, water pumps, and coolant quality issues reduce reliability margin on a forced-induction engine.",
      "Upper-engine oil leaks from cam cover and surrounding sealing areas become common with age and should not be normalized.",
      "Engine mounts often collapse with age and can make the four-cylinder drivetrain feel harsher than the engine itself really is.",
      "Poor previous maintenance is common because many buyers assume the E 200 Kompressor is a simple entry-level engine and delay the baseline work it still needs."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability of the M271.946 is that it can still feel usable even when timing, ignition, or intake problems are already developing.",
      "Oil quality and interval discipline are major vulnerabilities because this engine depends more heavily on clean oil than older Mercedes naturally aspirated units.",
      "Timing-chain health is a real vulnerability because neglected servicing can turn a manageable early symptom into a much more expensive repair.",
      "Crankcase ventilation health is a major vulnerability because a tired system can quietly affect oil behavior, idle quality, and long-term intake cleanliness.",
      "Low-quality aftermarket ignition, sensor, and timing parts are a major vulnerability because the engine loses refinement quickly when poor parts are installed.",
      "Supercharger drive-condition is a hidden vulnerability because belt and related wear can gradually reduce the engine's intended response without immediate dramatic failure.",
      "Cooling-system complacency is dangerous because this is still a forced-induction aluminum-head petrol engine with less tolerance for neglect than older larger naturally aspirated engines.",
      "Mount neglect is a hidden vulnerability because many owners blame the engine for harshness that is actually caused by collapsed mounts.",
      "Weak service history is especially dangerous because many used examples were bought as affordable premium cars and maintained to a price.",
      "This engine is also vulnerable to long service intervals, which are often less kind to ageing used examples than shorter preventive maintenance."
    ],
