    yearRange: "2005-2009",
    timingType: "Simplex roller chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. Unlike the immortal double-row chains of previous inline-six diesels, this chain is prone to stretching at higher mileages (usually beyond 250,000 km). Preventative replacement of the hydraulic chain tensioner can prolong its life, but a stretched chain will eventually require a significant replacement job to avoid jumping teeth.",
    injectionType: "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) utilizing Bosch piezo-electric injectors operating at up to 1600 bar. This extremely high pressure ensures microscopic fuel atomization for maximum torque and lower emissions. The Piezo injectors are incredibly sensitive to fuel quality and are highly expensive to replace if they fail electronically or suffer from poor spray patterns.",
    engineLifespan: "250 000 - 450 000+ km. The aluminum block with cast-iron cylinder liners is structurally incredibly robust. The true lifespan of the engine is almost entirely dictated by the maintenance of its complex peripheral systems. If the notorious oil cooler seals leak and are ignored, or if the DPF system clogs and causes excessive backpressure, the engine will suffer premature failure.",
    turboType: "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) / Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VGT) mounted in the valley of the V6 engine. It provides incredibly fast spool times and eliminates turbo lag. The electronic turbo actuator is prone to failure from extreme heat cycling, and the turbo intake seal is notorious for leaking oil directly onto the swirl flap motor.",
    ecuType: "Bosch EDC16CP31. A highly sophisticated engine control unit managing the Piezo injectors, VGT turbocharger, and the exhaust aftertreatment system. For manual transmission variants, this ECU artificially limits engine torque to 400 Nm (down from 440 Nm) to protect the dual-mass flywheel and manual gearbox components from excessive low-end strain.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are identical to the more powerful E 320 CDI. However, increasing power on a manual E 280 CDI will very quickly exceed the torque capacity of the dual-mass flywheel, leading to immediate clutch slip and catastrophic flywheel chatter.",
