    "fuelType": "Petrol",
    "cylinders": 6,
    "valves": 18,
    "compression": "10.5:1",
    "yearRange": "2002-2005",
    "timingType": "Single overhead camshaft per cylinder bank, 3-valve-per-cylinder, double-roller-chain-driven naturally aspirated V6 timing layout. The M112.913 is one of the last old-school Mercedes V6s: simple by later standards, smooth in a relaxed way, and more durability-focused than performance-led. In the W211 E240 it feels refined and honest rather than fast, and it suits drivers who value mechanical simplicity over headline output.",
    "injectionType": "Microprocessor-controlled multi-point manifold fuel injection with hot-film air-mass sensing and Bosch ME engine management. The M112 does not depend on complicated direct-injection hardware or turbocharging, which is a big part of why it has such a strong long-term reputation. When healthy, it delivers smooth idle quality, predictable throttle response, and very consistent everyday drivability.",
    "engineLifespan": "270,000 to 450,000 km. The M112.913 is widely regarded as one of Mercedes-Benz's more durable V6 petrol engines, and real-world ownership cost usually comes from ageing seals, intake and breather issues, cooling components, and neglected ignition service rather than catastrophic bottom-end weakness. A well-kept example can cover very high mileage with relatively straightforward maintenance.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. The M112.913 does not offer strong low-end punch by modern standards, but it delivers its power in a smooth, linear, and predictable way. Its appeal is long-term mechanical simplicity, cultured running, and a relaxed executive-car character rather than outright speed.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME engine management with hot-film air-mass measurement, adaptive fueling, ignition control, and manifold-injection calibration. This system is much simpler than later Mercedes direct-injection setups, which helps long-term serviceability. A healthy engine feels stable and consistent, while vacuum leaks, breather faults, or neglected ignition parts make it feel older very quickly.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 3 / market-dependent configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "190-200 hp. The M112.913 has very limited tuning value because it is a naturally aspirated durability-focused V6. The sensible ownership path is maintaining the engine in top condition rather than chasing small gains that do not materially change performance.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "15.5-15.9 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.8-8.1 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.8-11.1 L/100km",
      "sporty": "13.0-16.5 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 8.0L 5W-40 or 0W-40 fully synthetic oil meeting suitable Mercedes-Benz petrol-engine approvals. Conservative oil changes are recommended because the M112 ages best on clean oil, especially as gasket seepage, breather wear, and timing-chain-tensioner ageing become more relevant with mileage.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Valve cover gasket seepage is common on ageing M112 engines and often becomes one of the first visible oil leaks.",
      "Front cover and upper-engine sealing leaks develop with age and can make the engine look worse than its actual internal condition.",
      "Crankcase breather and ventilation-system ageing can increase oil consumption, create false air, and worsen engine sweating.",
      "The variable intake manifold linkage and related vacuum control hardware can become brittle, stick, or fail, affecting torque delivery and mixture behavior.",
      "Vacuum hoses become hard and brittle with age, causing rougher idle, fuel-trim drift, and inconsistent throttle response.",
      "Neglected spark plugs can shorten ignition-coil life because the M112 uses two spark plugs per cylinder.",
      "Thermostats can stick open on ageing engines, causing low operating temperature, weaker cabin heat, and higher fuel consumption.",
      "Water pump and coolant-system ageing should be taken seriously because old pumps and coolant leaks can eventually lead to overheating.",
      "Camshaft adjuster solenoid leak issues can allow oil migration into the engine harness if ignored.",
      "Engine mounts often collapse with age and can make the otherwise smooth V6 feel harsher than it really is."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability of the M112.913 is that buyers assume it is indestructible and then ignore age-related maintenance.",
      "Oil leaks are a real vulnerability because this engine family often remains mechanically strong while external sealing degrades steadily over time.",
      "Breather and vacuum-system condition is a major vulnerability because small air leaks can quietly spoil drivability without making the engine feel obviously broken.",
      "The intake-manifold linkage and vacuum control system are weak points because their ageing changes how the engine feels long before total failure.",
      "Ignition neglect is a vulnerability because the twin-plug setup rewards timely service and punishes owners who stretch plug intervals too far.",
      "Cooling-system complacency is dangerous because thermostat drift and old water pumps can slowly reduce reliability margin.",
      "Cam-adjuster solenoid oil leakage is especially important because it can contaminate wiring if left unresolved.",
      "Cheap aftermarket seals, breather parts, and ignition components are a vulnerability because the engine loses its core smoothness quickly when repaired badly.",
      "Weak service history is still risky, even on an M112, because simplicity does not cancel out years of neglected maintenance.",
      "This engine is also vulnerable to being underdiagnosed, where owners accept oil leaks, weak idle quality, and extra fuel consumption as normal ageing instead of fixing the actual causes."
    ],
