    "compression": "10.0:1",
    "yearRange": "2002-2005",
    "timingType": "Single overhead camshaft per cylinder bank, 3-valve-per-cylinder, double-roller-chain-driven naturally aspirated V6 timing layout. The M112.949 is the 3.2-liter version of Mercedes' older-school M112 V6 family, and in the pre-facelift W211 E320 it delivers the kind of smooth, relaxed, torque-rich character that suits a classic executive sedan extremely well. Compared with the smaller E240, it feels noticeably more effortless and better matched to the size and weight of the W211, especially in everyday driving and long-distance use. It is not a high-strung or flashy engine, but it is one of those Mercedes drivetrains that earns respect through consistency, durability, and an honest mechanical feel.",
    "injectionType": "Microprocessor-controlled multi-point manifold fuel injection with hot-film air-mass sensing and Bosch ME engine management. The M112 does not rely on direct injection, forced induction, or highly complicated later-era petrol hardware, and that mechanical simplicity is a big part of why it has such a strong long-term reputation. In good condition it feels smooth, predictable, and mechanically coherent, with strong low-to-mid-range usability rather than dramatic top-end excitement. The engine responds well to proper baseline maintenance, and when the intake, breather, vacuum, and ignition systems are healthy, it delivers very stable day-to-day drivability.",
    "engineLifespan": "300,000 to 500,000 km. The M112.949 is widely seen as one of Mercedes-Benz's more dependable naturally aspirated V6 petrol engines, and most real-world ownership cost comes from ageing seals, breather and vacuum issues, cooling-system wear, ignition service, and ordinary chassis-related ageing rather than major bottom-end weakness. A good example can cover very high mileage without drama if it receives sensible fluid changes and age-related preventive maintenance. A neglected one usually does not fail in spectacular fashion, but instead becomes untidy and expensive through several medium-level faults that were allowed to stack up over time.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. The M112.949 does not chase big headline performance, but it gives the pre-facelift E320 a relaxed, muscular, and very usable torque curve for real-road driving. It feels more substantial and more effortless than the smaller M112 2.6, and it suits the W211's luxury-touring character much better. Its appeal is smoothness, torque delivery, and durability rather than outright speed.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME engine management with adaptive fueling, ignition control, hot-film air-mass measurement, and manifold-injection calibration. This system is simpler and generally easier to live with long-term than later Mercedes petrol setups, which helps the M112 preserve its reputation as a durable ownership engine. A healthy car feels stable, smooth, and mechanically tidy. When vacuum leaks, breather issues, oil seepage, or neglected ignition parts enter the picture, the engine usually still runs, but it loses the composed feel that makes it attractive in the first place.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 3 / market-dependent configuration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "235-245 hp. The M112.949 is not really a tuning-led engine in this application because its value lies in smoothness, torque, and long-term durability rather than chasing modest naturally aspirated gains. The sensible approach is keeping the engine mechanically excellent rather than trying to transform it into something it was never designed to be.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "15.2-15.8 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.6-8.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.6-11.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "13.0-17.0 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 8.0L 5W-40 or 0W-40 fully synthetic oil meeting suitable Mercedes-Benz petrol-engine approvals. Conservative oil changes are recommended because even a durable engine like the M112 benefits heavily from clean oil as mileage rises, especially for seal life, top-end cleanliness, chain-tension behavior, and long-term overall smoothness. On older W211s this matters because many surviving cars are mechanically strong but have been serviced too casually.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Valve cover gasket seepage is common on ageing M112 engines and is often one of the first visible leak points.",
      "Front timing cover and upper-engine sealing leaks can develop with age and create an engine that looks messier than its real internal condition.",
      "Crankcase breather and ventilation-system ageing can increase oil use, worsen oil seepage, and contribute to false-air behavior.",
      "Vacuum hoses become hard and brittle with age, which can affect idle quality, fuel trims, and general smoothness.",
      "The variable intake manifold linkage and related vacuum-control hardware can wear, stick, or fail, affecting torque delivery and mixture behavior.",
      "Neglected spark plugs can shorten ignition-coil life because the M112 uses two spark plugs per cylinder.",
      "Thermostat ageing is common and can cause lazy warm-up, lower operating temperature, weaker cabin heat, and higher fuel consumption.",
      "Water pump and general cooling-system ageing should be taken seriously because old pumps, hoses, and coolant neglect reduce reliability margin.",
      "Camshaft adjuster solenoid leak issues can allow oil migration into the engine harness if ignored.",
      "Engine mounts often collapse with age and can make the otherwise smooth V6 feel harsher and more tired than it really is."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability of the M112.949 is that buyers hear it is a durable Mercedes V6 and then assume age-related maintenance no longer matters.",
      "Oil leaks are a real vulnerability because the basic engine often remains mechanically strong while external sealing steadily deteriorates with time.",
      "Breather and vacuum-system condition is a major vulnerability because small air leaks quietly spoil drivability without always making the engine feel obviously broken.",
      "The intake-manifold linkage and vacuum-control system are weak points because ageing here changes the engine's character long before full failure.",
      "Ignition neglect is a vulnerability because the twin-plug setup rewards proper service and punishes owners who stretch plug intervals too far.",
      "Cooling-system complacency is dangerous because thermostat drift and water-pump ageing slowly reduce reliability margin without dramatic early symptoms.",
      "Cam-adjuster solenoid oil leakage matters because it can contaminate wiring and create more expensive electrical issues if ignored.",
      "Cheap aftermarket seals, breather parts, and ignition components are a vulnerability because the engine loses its smoothness quickly when repaired badly.",
      "Weak service history is still risky, even on an M112, because simplicity does not cancel years of poor maintenance.",
      "This engine is also vulnerable to being underdiagnosed, where owners accept oil sweating, weak idle quality, or extra fuel use as normal ageing instead of addressing the real causes."
    ],
